<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chambéry</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chambéry is a city in the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, historic capital of Savoy, set between the Bauges and Chartreuse massifs. The counts of Savoy bought it in 1232, made it their capital in 1295 and kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shroud-of-turin">Holy Shroud</a> in its castle chapel from 1502 until 1578. It lost its role when Emmanuel Philibert moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1563, and it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with Savoy in 1860.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chambery.jpg" alt="Chambéry" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florian Pépellin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chamb%C3%A9ry_-_Place_St-L%C3%A9ger.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chambéry was founded at a crossroads of ancient routes between the Dauphiné, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in a wide valley between the Bauges and Chartreuse massifs on the Leysse River. It lies 214 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, 100 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and 85 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, and is a railway and road junction. Its surroundings run from the vineyard slopes of the Combe de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savoie">Savoie</a> almost to the shores of the Lac du Bourget, the largest natural lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The history of Chambéry is closely linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>. It was the Savoyard capital from 1295 to 1563, when Savoy stretched from Bourg-en-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>. The Château des ducs de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savoie">Savoie</a>, a fortified castle dating from the 11th century, was settled by the first counts of Savoy in 1285 and expanded in the early 14th century as residence, seat of administration and stronghold. It soon became obsolete as a defence against a siege, and constant French hostilities led Duke Emmanuel Philibert to move his capital to Turin in 1563.</p><p>The château remained an administrative centre until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christine-of-france">Christine Marie of France</a>, Duchess of Savoy, returned to hold court there in 1640. In 1684 it saw the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> and Anne Marie d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. Victor Amadeus III added a Royal Wing in 1786. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the lands of the duchy west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in 1792, but returned them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1815 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat. Chambéry was ceded to France by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> in 1860, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, and its Hôtel de Ville opened in 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chambéry is the prefecture and largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savoie">Savoie</a> department, with 60,251 inhabitants in 2022. The château now houses the departmental administration. The Fontaine des Éléphants, built in 1838 to honour Benoît de Boigne's feats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, is its best-known landmark. The Cistercian Abbey of Hautecombe, founded in 1135, is one of the burial places of the rulers of Savoy, and the cathedral of Saint Francis of Sales has the largest trompe-l'oeil paintings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Sisters of St Joseph, an order founded at Chambéry in 1812, work in teaching and charity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chambéry” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count Amadeus VIII bought the County of Geneva, the Genevois, in 1401, and on 19 February 1416 Emperor Sigismund, visiting <b>Chambéry</b>, raised him to the rank of duke, making Savoy an imperial duchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Peter II, called the 'little Charlemagne', won the Vaud in the 13th century, and <b>Chambéry</b> became the capital in 1295.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-savoy">County of Savoy</a></li></ul></section>
